- "task:B77": {
-  "evidence_line": "The claim about the live code was re-checked 26 days after the designs were written and still holds: the convergence check takes 10 inputs, 0 naming the roster, and the stricter gate takes 3, also 0. The degraded-round rate is 3 of 53 runs, 0.056604, with a 95 percent interval from 0.019437 to 0.153702. The 2 repairs were shown identical at a constant roster symbolically and by enumeration, and divergent on a varying one by worked example. My first attempt to measure the degraded rate returned 0 of 0 and that was the instrument rather than the answer. The third design's defect claim is quantified rather than described: the live check returns the same answer with an identical stated reason for a 6-seat and a 2-seat quiet window, and at the measured rates the chance of a falsely quiet window is 0.115873 at 6 seats against 0.287325 at 2. I withdrew a concern that it closes too permissively when no findings arrive at all: computing the sequence rather than sampling 1 point showed its guard matches the limit exactly.",
-  "options": [
-   "**Adopt the exact version**, which is my recommendation on the simplest sufficient and additive standards. It is 1 file, it holds the risk at or below the full-roster value at every roster size from 1 to 6, it costs a healthy run nothing, and its case for combining its 2 parts is the only measured one in this arc. Its reasoning is cross-checked 2 ways inside the design, agreeing with an independent numerical calculation to better than 1 part in 1,000,000,000, and a constraint solver confirms its 2 labels cannot both apply and that the quiet count alone cannot tell the 2 causes apart.",
-   "**Adopt the safe upper bound instead**, in the seat-rounds form, if you would rather not depend on 2 measured numbers at all. It is never too small and it stays defined when the roster changes between rounds, at the cost of up to 9 extra quiet rounds at a very small roster.",
-   "**Leave it unadopted** and accept that a run losing seats may close sooner than its pre-registered threshold intended. The status quo, and not neutral: at 2 live seats the chance of a falsely quiet window is 2.4796 times the full-roster value, and the gate cannot see the difference."
-  ],
-  "spoken_heading": "WHETHER THE CONVERGENCE GATE SHOULD KNOW HOW MANY SEATS ARE ANSWERING",
-  "still_owed": "a decision on whether to adopt the exact roster-aware requirement or the safe upper bound, and if the exact one, which of the 2 recorded correlation figures it should use: the pooled 0.405989 or the within-experiment 0.236014, where the gate is a within-run decision and the design currently defaults to the pooled one. It changes the requirement at 2 of 6 roster sizes.",
-  "title": "B77 \u2014 whether to adopt the roster-aware convergence window, which requires a run missing seats to stay quiet for proportionally longer before it may close",
-  "what": "The live convergence check takes 10 parameters and not 1 of them names the roster. That matters because 1 of its 2 sides counts consecutive rounds in which no new critical finding arrived, and that count gets easier to satisfy as the roster shrinks: fewer seats generate fewer findings, and the gate cannot tell a quiet healthy panel from a quiet depleted panel. An unadopted seat design written on 2026-10-07 repairs it by scaling the requirement, so a run missing seats has to stay quiet for proportionally longer before it may close. Its reasoning was checked rather than taken on trust, in 4 steps: the bound never asks for less than the exact requirement at any level of correlation between seats, 0 violations over 12,000 combinations, and it is exact rather than merely safe when seats are independent. 1 precondition the design does not state was found during that check, and is recorded with it: a panel whose critical rate is 0 gives an undefined requirement, and the design covers only the case of an empty roster. The case it addresses is real rather than hypothetical. 3 of 53 archived runs carry a round in which fewer seats answered than were taking part, and 1 of those is the failure of 18 July that the runner's own code describes in a comment. A SECOND REPAIR FOR THE SAME DEFECT WAS FOUND ON 2026-11-02 AND IT IS THE DESIGN NOW RECOMMENDED. A different seat, writing to the same brief on 2026-10-07, repaired it by counting quiet SEAT-ROUNDS rather than quiet rounds: the requirement becomes the roster size times the round count, and each round contributes however many seats actually answered. At a full roster, and at any roster that stays the same size, the 2 rules are literally the same rule, confirmed symbolically and over every roster size from 1 to 6 with 0 disagreements. They come apart when the roster CHANGES between rounds, which is the only shape the archive actually holds: all 3 degraded runs have a single short round with full rounds on at least 1 side. The first rule takes a single number for how many seats are live, so a run whose roster changed has to be given 1 value and the rule does not say which, and the 2 sensible choices disagree by as much as 6 rounds. On a run that lost 4 of 6 seats for 1 round and recovered, the second rule asks for 5 quiet rounds, while the first asks for 9 if you take the worst round and 3 if you take the last. The second rule has no such choice to make. It also aims at a bigger target: the stricter of the 2 convergence gates, which is the gate in force, has a fallback path where closing rests on the quiet count alone, and that gate takes 3 inputs of which none names the roster. A THIRD DESIGN WAS FOUND ON 2026-11-02 AND IT IS THE DESIGN NOW RECOMMENDED, which means this recommendation has changed twice. The same seat that wrote the first design also wrote a second design in the same brief, and that second design is the more complete. The 3 are not 3 rival answers once seen side by side. The first states the exact requirement and then takes a safe upper bound on it, which is why it needs no measured inputs. The third computes that same exact requirement, which costs it a dependence on 2 measured numbers. The second, from the other seat, restates the upper bound in a form that stays defined when the roster changes between rounds. The bound is never too small, checked over 36 combinations of roster size and correlation, and it is substantially looser: at a 1-seat roster it demands 18 quiet rounds where the exact requirement is 9 to 11, and at 2 seats 9 against 6 to 7. Every extra round is an extra round of every seat's time, and on a paid roster it is paid. WHAT DECIDES IT IS THAT THE THIRD DESIGN'S CASE FOR COMBINING ITS 2 PARTS IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED, and it is the only such case found all day. Its own committed falsifier runs clean and shows each part curing a failure the other cannot: with only the record of who answered, the run is labelled degraded but still closes