+**B81. The commit gate costs more than the limit written in its own header, and nothing enforces that limit.** Added 2026-11-02T17:43:49+00:00. **WHAT THIS IS, in plain terms.** Every commit in this project runs a set of fast correctness tests first, and refuses the commit if any are red. That set is `hooks/pre-commit`. Its own header states the design limit in these words: *"A per-commit gate costing 11 minutes would be bypassed within a day, and a guard that gets bypassed is not a guard."* **11 minutes is 660 seconds. MEASURED: the commit made on 2026-11-02 took 769 seconds, and the figure the gate had recorded before that was 800 seconds.** Those are 1.1652 and 1.2121 times the stated limit. The gate's own cost producer, `scripts/precommit_gate_cost_2026-09-10.py`, states at its line 22 that the gate *"takes over 11 minutes"*, so the overrun is not news to the code either. **WHAT IT AFFECTS.** 2 things, and only 2, which is why the blast radius is small and the cost is nonetheless real. `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py` derives its commit subprocess timeout from the recorded duration, which is a sound design and already protects the save path: a hard-coded 60-second timeout broke `sv` entirely on 2026-10-17 when the gate had grown to 143 seconds, and deriving it fixed that. The second is wall-clock: at 769 seconds every commit costs nearly 13 minutes of the founder's machine, which is a fixed resource of 8 cores and 8 GiB. **WHY IT GREW, and the cause is structural rather than careless.** The header says so itself: `test_done_markers_carry_evidence` parametrises per completed task entry, so **the gate's cost grows with this very list**. It is not a fixed price. The header's own measurement of 277 tests in a median 7.09 seconds was taken when the guard list held 7 files. It now holds about 125. **WHAT IS ACTUALLY MISSING.** Not the measurement, which exists and is accurate. Searching the tree, exactly 2 files read `.cdsfl_tmp/precommit_seconds`: the hook that writes it and `sv` that consumes it. **No mechanism compares the measured duration against the 660-second limit the header states.** So the project has written down a standard, measured itself exceeding it, and has nothing that would ever say so. That is the symmetric half of the additive standard: a rule nothing reaches. **RECOMMENDATION, against the 3 formal standards. Simplest sufficient:** a guard that reads the recorded duration and the stated limit from the header itself, and fails when the first exceeds the second. It needs no new figure, because both numbers already exist in files. **Additive:** no guard is removed and no test is dropped; what is added is a loud signal that the set has outgrown its tier, which is the information needed to split it deliberately into a commit-time tier and a periodic tier rather than having it bypassed silently. **No composability claim is made:** the limit check alone is sufficient, and pairing it with a second repair would not dominate it on any named property. **WHAT IS NOT RECOMMENDED, and the reason matters.** Lowering the cost by deleting guards. The record is unambiguous: on 2026-09-09 a commit reached HEAD with the suite red at 5 failures because ordering by discipline failed within an hour of being written down, and `hooks/pre-commit` is what replaced that discipline. **RELATED:** B36, which found a 300-second global timeout silently discarding longer budgets, and B35, which found the hook watching 9.58% of the test files. **NEEDS HIS RULING:** whether to add a guard that fails when the commit gate exceeds the 660-second limit its own header states, and therefore whether to split the guard set into a commit-time tier and a periodic tier. It is his and not mine because the guard would start refusing commits the moment it exists, the gate already standing at 745 seconds. **THE SERIES IS NOT YET REPRODUCIBLE AND THAT IS STATED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER.** `.cdsfl_tmp/precommit_seconds` holds only the LAST duration and is scratch, not committed, so the 4 figures were read serially as each commit finished and cannot be recomputed from the repository. The mean and the t-test therefore rest on observations recorded by hand, which `measured-rate-travels-with-its-script` treats as a claim about evidence rather than evidence. **Making the series reproducible is part of what this entry asks for**: the hook would append each duration to a committed ledger instead of overwriting 1 number, which costs nothing and turns the limit check into something any later session can verify. ★ **THE DOMINANT COST IS A DIFFERENT GUARD FROM THE ONE THE HEADER BLAMES, MEASURED 2026-11-02T19:13:46+00:00.** The hook's header attributes the growth to `test_done_markers_carry_evidence`, which parametrises per completed task entry. That is real and it is the smaller driver. **`test_scripts_are_reached_2026-09-11.py` scans the WHOLE TRACKED TREE, and its input is the repository rather than a fixed file set.** Measured: **11,116 tracked files, 572 scripts whose reachedness is checked, 10,705 file bodies to scan, 505,437,625 bytes = 482.02 MiB**, giving **6,123,260 name-by-body pairs** and a BOUND of **2.891103e+11 bytes = 269.25 GiB of string search** if every name had to be searched over every body — NumPy and mpmath agreeing to 12 significant figures. **THAT BOUND IS NOT THE WORK DONE, and the distinction is from reading the loop rather than from timing it:** the inner loop BREAKS on the first vouching body that mentions a script, so a reached script stops early and only a genuinely unreached one pays the full pass. With matches falling on average part-way through 10,705 bodies, the realised work is on the order of half the bound rather than the bound, which is consistent with the CPU time observed. **What is unconditional is reading and normalising all 482.02 MiB once**; what is variable is how far into the bodies each of the 572 names has to look. **OBSERVED COST, not inferred: 4 minutes 28 seconds of CPU and still running** when this was written, against **51.54 seconds** for an 8-file guard set that excluded it. **So adding 1 script to `scripts/` adds a full pass over every tracked body**, which is why the gate's cost rises with work that has nothing to do with the task list. **WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE RECOMMENDATION.** Splitting the guard set into tiers is still right, but it is no longer the only lever and it is not the cheapest. **Building the body index ONCE and searching all 572 names against it turns O(names x bodies) into O(bodies)**, which is a pure performance change that should leave the result set byte-identical. **IT IS NOT BUILT HERE AND THAT IS DELIBERATE: a performance change to a RATCHET must be shown to preserve its verdict set before it is applied**, because an optimisation that silently drops a match would loosen the ratchet by exactly the width of what it dropped, and the ratchet's own guard `test_the_recorded_set_is_still_accurate` only catches movement in 1 direction. The evidence that would license it is the 2 verdict sets compared element-by-element, not a timing improvement. ★ **THE GATE'S OTHER HALF IS COVERAGE, AND IT IS MEASURED 2026-11-02T19:57:25+00:00 BECAUSE A RED GUARD WAS FOUND OUTSIDE IT TONIGHT.** **MEASURED: `hooks/pre-commit`'s guard list holds 120 files against 785 test files tracked under `bench/tests` — 15.2866%, Wilson 95% [0.129388, 0.179726], Clopper-Pearson [0.128397, 0.179975].** So **665 test files are never run by `hooks/pre-commit`, which is what refuses a red commit.** **THE INSTANCE THAT SURFACED IT.** `bench/tests/test_blocker_triage_2026-09-10.py` holds 21 tests on the triage tool and is 1 of the 665. Running it by hand on 2026-11-02 found 1 labelled case red, and bisection across `a59fc3ac`, `cf215aa8`, `0414d6f9`, `c1ea2443` and `9a5c06aa` puts the flip at the 5th — so it had been red for 5 commits with nothing to notice. **The cause was a legitimate change in the RECORD rather than in the predicate**: `9a5c06aa` added `[NEEDS YOUR RULING]` to B21, which already discussed `max_rounds` twice, so an item about `max_rounds` defaulting wrongly genuinely does wait on B21's ruling now. HEAD's unedited tool reproduces the verdict, which is how the 2 repairs made to the tool the same evening were ruled out as the cause. **WHY THIS IS NOT AN ARGUMENT FOR ADDING ALL 665, and that is the point for his decision.** The gate already costs 745 s, and its dominant guard scans the whole tracked tree. **Cost and coverage trade against each other directly**, so the 4 options above are really a choice about WHICH 120-odd of 785 files the gate should watch and at what price — not merely about how long it may take. **The 2 figures that frame that choice are now both measured: 745 s and 15.2866%.** **AND 1 THING IS CLEAR WITHOUT HIS RULING: a guard that exists only to keep an instrument honest is worth little outside the gate.** The triage tool decides whether work stops; its guard was unwatched for 5 commits. Whether that specific file joins the list is still part of the same decision, because adding it is not free. ★ **THE LEDGER IS BUILT, 2026-11-08, AND THE GUARD THAT WOULD REFUSE ON THE LIMIT IS NOT — BECAUSE IT WOULD BLOCK EVERY COMMIT TODAY.** His added condition is largely ALREADY satisfied and that is reported rather than claimed as new: `hooks/pre-commit` already extracts the failing tests and prints them, so a refusal from a red guard already says which guard. What was missing is a refusal from the LIMIT, because there was no limit to refuse from: the hook recorded its duration by OVERWRITING 1 scratch number, so the gate had no cost HISTORY and the limit stated in its own header — 11 minutes, so 660 seconds — had nothing to be checked against. **MEASURED FROM THIS SESSION'S OWN BOARDS: 652, 683 and 698 seconds, so the gate sits ON that line rather than inside it.** The exit trap now APPENDS a row carrying the duration, the HEAD and the trap's own exit code, so the refusal rate becomes derivable — a refusal previously left NO trace in the repository, which is why this session's refusal count had to be session-observed. The single scratch number is KEPT, because `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py:620` derives its own timeout from that exact path. Producer: `scripts/what_the_commit_gate_actually_costs_over_time_2026-11-08.py`; guard: `bench/tests/test_the_gate_records_its_own_cost_2026-11-08.py`, 8 tests, 1 of which EXECUTES the hook's printf in a subshell and parses its output, because a format error would otherwise sit in the hook until someone first read the ledger. **WHY THE REFUSAL IS NOT BUILT:** the gate is AT its stated limit, so a check that refused on it would block every commit until the guard set is split into a commit-time tier and a periodic one — and that split, with the bound it is split against, is **B103**, which this list already records as 1 decision wearing 2 numbers. **[NEEDS HIS RULING] on B103's bound, which is the same ruling this entry waits on.** ★★★ **THE LIMIT IS NOW EXCEEDED BY A FACTOR OF 2.41, AND IT WAS ALREADY EXCEEDED BEFORE TONIGHT AT p = 0.022365.** Measured 2026-11-09 after B103 put every guard dated 2026-10-29 or later on the gate, 128 files to 232 and 3,942 tests collected. Producer: `scripts/what_the_commit_gate_actually_costs_over_time_2026-11-08.py` -- run it with `--max-seconds 900 --add-seconds 890.45`. **IT REPORTED THE GATE OVER ITS STATED LIMIT ON 7 OF 8 RECORDED RUNS WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN ON 2026-11-08: 0.875000, Wilson [0.529112, 0.977583], Clopper-Pearson [0.473490, 0.996840], both routes agreeing to 1.11e-16. ★ RE-RUN 2026-11-09 IT REPORTS 12 OF 13, 0.923077, Wilson [0.666860, 0.986290], Clopper-Pearson [0.639703, 0.998054], AND THE PAST TENSE IS NOW DELIBERATE.** The ledger grew from 8 rows to 14 because the boards of this session's own commits appended to the very file being measured — the same self-measurement that forced the withdrawal recorded in the next sentence, reappearing in the denominator rather than in the mean. The conclusion is unchanged and slightly stronger; what changed is that a figure written in the present tense about a growing ledger goes stale by being correct at the time. **1 of those 14 rows is NOT a clean measurement and is KEPT rather than pruned:** 705 s, exit 0, 2026-11-09T14:39:16Z, written by a board that a test invoked against the live repository while the GUARDS list it reads was being rewritten. Dropping it gives 11 of 12, 0.916667, Wilson [0.646120, 0.985135], Clopper-Pearson [0.615204, 0.997892], so it carries no argument either way, and `never-delete-bulk-resources` says point at it rather than remove it. ★★ **AND A SIGNIFICANCE CLAIM MADE EARLIER THE SAME NIGHT IS WITHDRAWN.** A second instrument of mine, written without checking that this one existed, computed mean 706.000000 s over 5 ledger rows and reported t = 2.886297, p = 0.022365, REJECT -- read as 'the gate was already significantly over its limit'. **On the fuller ledger the mean is 675.833333 s over the 6 boards at or under 900 s, sd 80.474634, t = 0.481936 on 5 degrees of freedom, p = 0.325103 by scipy and mpmath agreeing, and the 95 per cent interval [591.380385, 760.286282] CONTAINS 660. CANNOT REJECT.** The 5-row sample was read at about 01:45 and **this session's own commit attempts then appended 4 more rows**, including a 525 s board that lowers the mean and widens the sd from 35.64 to 80.474634: the act of committing was changing the population being measured. The 7 of 8 figure above is the frame that survives, because it does not depend on the mean of a sample being stable. The duplicate script is RETIRED and its 2 unique contributions -- the t-test and the projection -- are folded into the incumbent, which was already guarded by `bench/tests/test_the_gate_records_its_own_cost_2026-11-08.py`. **THE 232-FILE BOARDS MEASURED 1,593.05 s AND 1,587.85 s**, and the projection from the bounded mean is 1,566.28 s = 26.10 min = 2.373157 times the stated limit. **1 short run of 12 s at exit 1 is NAMED AND EXCLUDED rather than averaged in**, because an infrastructure refusal is not an observation of what a board costs and including it would make an expensive gate look cheap. **THE 232-FILE BOARD MEASURED 1,593.05 s AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT POOLED WITH THOSE 5:** it is a different configuration, and pooling 2 configurations measures 2 things at once, which is the ecological confound this project corrected elsewhere. Against the stated limit that single observation is a factor of 2.413712, and it came in 0.002130 relative to a projection of 1,596.45 s made before the run. **WHAT IS OWED IS HIS, AND IT IS THE LIMIT RATHER THAN THE ADDITION:** he ruled the addition with the 26-minute figure in hand, so it stands. 3 options are recorded in `hooks/pre-commit` and NONE is recommended, because nothing measures any of them against the others and `composability-requires-a-measurement` forbids offering a pair without that evidence: raise the stated limit to match what he accepted; split the list into a fast tier gating every commit and a slow tier gating a push; or run the slow tier concurrently, which spends his 8 cores and 8 GiB and is therefore his call. B103 is closed on its addition and hands this half here. ★★★ **A 4TH AND 5TH MEASUREMENT OF THE GATE'S COST, 2026-11-10, AND THE 2ND ONE IS A DIFFERENT FAILURE MODE FROM THE COST ITSELF.** The gate ran to completion at **1791.23 s, 29 minutes 51 seconds**, for 4,033 passed and 12 skipped with 0 failures — against the 660 s its own header states, so **2.71 times the stated limit**. **AND IT BROKE `sv` BEFORE IT BROKE ANYTHING ELSE:** `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py` runs the commit through `subprocess.run(..., timeout=commit_timeout(root))`, which expired at **300 s** and raised `TimeoutExpired`, so `sv --commit --push` exited 1 with no commit and a traceback. **WORSE, THE TIMEOUT KILLED THE `git commit` AND NOT THE HOOK'S PROCESS GROUP**, so the board kept running ORPHANED, reparented to pid 1, burning a core it could never