-**B94. The 2 decisions left over from the verified-flag repair: what `v_bar` is supposed to measure, and what the 4 sibling runners should do about a close path with no execution behind it.** Added 2026-11-03T00:36:00+00:00. **STATUS: MEASURED. NEEDS HIS RULING: on both halves. Raised as its own entry DELIBERATELY.** **WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM B91 RATHER THAN A PARAGRAPH INSIDE IT.** B91's repair is DONE and COMMITTED, so B91 carries `state: DONE`, and the morning report builds its decisions section from entries that are still OPEN. A decision recorded inside a closed entry is therefore invisible on the 1 surface that exists to show him decisions -- the same shape as the 4th instance of 2 surfaces reporting different numbers, and the reason the rule is "pin a set, not a count". Splitting them out is the fix. **DECISION 1: WHAT `v_bar` MEASURES, AND WHETHER THE PARSER SHOULD READ A MODEL'S OWN VERIFIED CLAIM AT ALL.** `bench/dm/_manager.py:717` computes `v_bar` as the fraction of a model's findings whose `verified` flag is true, and `bench/dm/_types.py:331` documents it as mean verification quality. It is 1 of the 4 dimensions of the CapabilityFingerprint, and `bench/dm/_role_assignment.py:81` scores a model as the dot product of a role's alpha vector with the normalised fingerprint, with `_tie_break_key` at `:85` placing `v_bar` second in a lexicographic order whose own docstring says higher values win. **So `v_bar` enters role assignment positively.** The flag it reads can be set either by an execution or by the model writing `"VERIFIED": true` in its own reply, and **nothing in the stored fingerprints records which setter produced a value**. The dimension is not inert -- **38 of 50 archived `v_bar` observations are nonzero, 0.760000, Wilson 95% [0.625873, 0.857026]** -- but because the setter is unrecorded, **no claim is made here that self-reporting has actually moved a role assignment**, and that boundary is deliberate rather than cautious phrasing. **THE QUESTION IS WHICH QUANTITY HE WANTS.** If `v_bar` is meant to measure how often a model's findings were VERIFIED BY THIS MACHINE, the parser should stop populating the flag from a model's reply and `v_bar` becomes a smaller, honest number. If it is meant to measure how often a model CLAIMS verification, it is measuring that correctly today and the name is wrong. **A third option exists and is the additive one:** keep both, as 2 fields, and let him choose which one the fingerprint reads. It was not taken unasked because changing what `v_bar` counts changes a measurement that drives the experiment. **DECISION 2: THE 4 SIBLING RUNNERS.** The same confirmed-to-closed gate reading the same flag sits at `bench/reference_runner.py:858`, `bench/run_exp35_policy_engine.py:678`, `bench/run_exp36_evidence.py:845` and `bench/run_exp37_evidence.py:851`. In all 4 the only setter of `verified` is `mark_verified`, and **none of the 4 writes `falsifier_verdict` or references `bugzilla_verified` anywhere**, so the precondition added to the canonical runner would make their close path unreachable instead of guarded -- disabling a feature, which the additive standard forbids. **This is the measured answer to his Q15 about folding fixes back: a tested fix does not always fold back.** v1 is not dead code -- 36 live files reference it by word boundary, `bench/immune_agents.py` and `bench/endocrine.py` among them. **THE MISLABEL IS NARROWER THAN THE DEFECT, which matters for how urgent this is:** v1 and its 3 siblings close with a bare `resolve(cid, "CLOSED", round_idx)` passing no adjudicator, so their closures are UNLABELLED rather than falsely attributed to a tool, which is why 840 of 1562 archived closures carry an empty `status_adjudicator`. The false `adjudicator="tool"` was v3 only and is fixed. **THE 3 OPTIONS FOR THE 4 SIBLINGS.** Give them an execution path, which means porting falsifier machinery into runners whose experiments have already completed. Or change their closure to record honestly what it rests on, which is a label change and not a behaviour change. Or leave them, on the ground that their experiments are finished and their archives are read rather than extended. **THE RECOMMENDATION, under the formal standards:** the label change, because it is the SIMPLEST SUFFICIENT change that removes the untruth without removing the feature, and because porting machinery into a completed experiment's runner adds a surface nothing will exercise, which the symmetric form of the additive standard treats as not additive. **A RATCHET IS ALREADY IN PLACE SO THIS CANNOT GO QUIET:** `bench/tests/test_a_models_word_does_not_close_a_finding_2026-11-03.py::TestWhyTheFixWasNotFoldedBack` fails if any of the 4 later gains a `falsifier_verdict` writer, at which point the fold-back becomes possible and the test says so. **AND THE SIZE OF THE EXPOSURE IS NOW DERIVED RATHER THAN LEFT AS 'NOT INERT'.** `_capability_score` is the dot product of a role's weight vector with the normalised fingerprint, and those weight vectors sum to 1 -- asserted, with NumPy and SymPy agreeing -- so the v_bar weight IS v_bar's share of the entire score. It is **0.350000 for PM, the orchestrator seat, 0.300000 for COL and 0.250000 for PAR**. `as_normalised_array` sets the term to `v_bar / pool_max`, so SymPy confirms symbolically that the maximum gain from inflating v_bar to the pool maximum is **exactly that weight**: `alpha1*v/m` at `v = m` minus the same at `v = 0` simplifies to `alpha1`. **AND IT IS WORSE THAN ADDITIVE, because the pool maximum is the DENOMINATOR.** A model that inflates its own v_bar BECOMES the pool maximum, so an honest model's term falls from 1.000000 to its own raw v_bar: an honest model at 0.800000 loses 0.070000 of the orchestrator score, 0.060000 of the classifier score and 0.050000 of the worker score. So normalising by the pool maximum both raises the self-reporter and depresses everyone else. **THIS IS DERIVED, NOT MEASURED, and the distinction is load-bearing:** it follows from the equations and says nothing about whether it has happened. Nothing in the stored fingerprints records which setter produced a v_bar, so no archived role assignment can be attributed to self-reporting, and none is. Producer: `scripts/what_a_self_reported_v_bar_is_worth_2026-11-03.py`. ★ **HIS RULING, 2026-11-08:** *"Capability finger printing is something the harness should do without HIL intervention. If your suggestion can be modified to ensure that happens, then prefer that option. fr"* — carried in from his annotated response file, read with `scripts/read_founder_responses_2026-10-11.py` and not from a recollection. **WHAT REMAINS IS A SCOPE QUESTION AND IS NOT PICKED HERE:** whether his acceptance reaches the second half, the 4 sibling runners' closure label, or only the v_bar half the index names. ★ **HIS QUESTION ANSWERED 2026-11-09, AND THE ANSWER IS YES WITH NO CHOICE LEFT FOR HIM TO MAKE.** His words: *"Capability finger printing is something the harness should do without HIL intervention. If your suggestion can be modified to ensure that happens, then prefer that option."* My recommendation had been to keep both values and let HIM choose which the fingerprint reads. **THAT WAS THE WRONG SHAPE AND HIS INSTRUCTION IS RIGHT: the rule already exists and needs no ruling.** The project's standing correction reads *"Findings confirmed programmatically or by HIL -- no model voting"*, so a model's own claim is not admissible evidence. **THE AUTOMATIC RULE IS THEREFORE: the fingerprint reads the MACHINE-VERIFIED count, always.** There is nothing to choose, because 1 of the 2 candidate values is already excluded by a rule he gave. **WHAT HAS TO CHANGE IN CODE.** `bench/dm/_manager.py:717` computes `v_bar` as `sum(1 for f in model_findings if f.verified) / len(model_findings)`, reading 1 field. That field can be set without a tool verification -- `scripts/a_models_own_verified_flag_closes_it_2026-11-03.py` found 34 of 412 closures resting