der 1 name, and no field distinguishes them: **0** occurrences of `verified_by`, `verified_claimed` or any equivalent. **WHY IT IS NOT A QUICK EDIT.** `v_bar` is 1 of the 4 dimensions of the capability fingerprint (`bench/dm/_types.py:321`, `(D_decay, v_bar, A, C)`), and that fingerprint feeds role assignment and the tie-break at `bench/dm/_role_assignment.py:92`. Changing what `v_bar` measures therefore changes which seat gets which role, which is a behaviour change on a scoring path rather than a field addition. Under `p-pass-high-consequence-check` that lands as its own commit with its own board, not bundled into a 35-file one. **AND ITS OWN PRODUCER NAMES A SEPARATE DECISION THAT IS HIS.** `scripts/a_models_own_verified_flag_closes_it_2026-11-03.py` runs at exit 0 and reports that the same close gate sits in 5 runners, that in the 4 siblings the ONLY setter is `mark_verified` and none of them writes `falsifier_verdict` or has a bugzilla path, so folding the v3 fix back mechanically *would make the close path unreachable rather than correct — disabling a feature, which the additive standard forbids*. What those 4 need is an execution path or an honest label, **and that is his ruling, not mine**. ★★★ **THE FACTUAL HALF OF DECISION 1 IS NOW SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT, 2026-11-10T17:26:46+00:00, AND THE DECISION ITSELF IS UNTOUCHED AND STILL HIS.** This entry says the `verified` flag *"can be set either by an execution or by the model writing `"VERIFIED": true` in its own reply"*. That is true of the PROJECT and false of the OBJECT `v_bar` reads. `bench/dm/_manager.py` computes `v_bar` by ATTRIBUTE access on the `Finding` dataclass, and walking syntax trees over `bench/` and `scripts/` finds **8** `Finding(..., verified=...)` construction sites of which **7 take their value from the model's reply and the 8th is a literal False** -- 0.875000, Wilson [0.529112, 0.977583], Clopper-Pearson [0.473490, 0.996840], 0 from anything else. **The execution path EXISTS as 18 dict assignments to `entry['verified']` on registry entries, which `v_bar` never reads.** So `v_bar` measures how often a model CLAIMS verification, the name is the thing that is wrong, and the 2 quantities already exist in 2 places and are simply not joined -- which makes his third, additive option cheaper than this entry assumed. **★ THE FIRST VERSION OF THAT MEASUREMENT WAS WRONG AND THE P-PASS CAUGHT IT.** Walking only constructor calls and attribute assignments gave "0 of 8 set it from an execution", read as *the project never verifies by execution*. Adding the 3 further routes -- dict subscript, `dataclasses.replace`, `setattr` -- returned 18 on the subscript route. A claim of 0 of N is only as strong as the set of places it looked, and that version had looked in 2 of 5. A second defect followed: the classifier decided by ENCLOSING FUNCTION, so `verified=False` was labelled a parsed reply because `verified_raw` appears elsewhere in the same function, and the literal tally then read 0 because it compared against a value expression it had already annotated. Producer: `scripts/what_v_bar_actually_counts_2026-11-10.py`. Guard: `bench/tests/test_what_v_bar_counts_is_measured_2026-11-10.py`, 15 tests, **6 of 6 mutations red**, with `scan()` parameterised over its roots so a planted site per route is findable -- without that, disabling the `setattr` branch left the guard GREEN, because an assertion that a route is ABSENT cannot tell a clean tree from a blind scanner. **NOTHING HERE DECIDES WHICH QUANTITY THE FINGERPRINT SHOULD READ**, because that changes a number driving the experiment.
