+  "task:B81": {
+    "evidence_line": "4 of 4 commits this session exceeded 660 s at 800, 769, 738 and 745 seconds, Wilson 95% [0.510109, 1.000000], Clopper-Pearson [0.397635, 1.000000]; n = 4 from 1 session on 1 machine, so this establishes the limit is exceeded here and not a population rate. The 4 durations were read serially from a scratch file that keeps only the last, so the series is not recomputable from the repository; making it so is part of what this asks for. The dominant cost is not the guard the header blames: test_scripts_are_reached scans the whole tracked tree, 11,116 files and 482.02 MiB, once per script name. And the gate watches 120 of 785 test files, 15.2866%, Wilson [0.129388, 0.179726]: a 21-test guard on the triage tool sat outside it and was red for 5 commits before being run by hand.",
+    "options": [
+      "**Add a guard that fails when the gate exceeds its own stated limit, and split the set into a commit-time tier and a periodic tier.** This is the recommendation. Simplest sufficient: the guard needs no new figure, because both numbers already exist in files; the same change should have the hook APPEND each duration to a committed ledger rather than overwrite 1 number, so the series becomes verifiable by any later session. Additive: no guard is removed and no test is dropped; what is added is a loud signal that the set has outgrown its tier. Note that it would start refusing commits immediately, which is the point — the split becomes deliberate rather than silent.",
+      "**Build the reachedness guard's body index once instead of per script name, and only then decide on tiers.** Measured: that guard scans 10,705 file bodies totalling 482.02 MiB for each of 572 script names, which bounds the work at 6,123,260 name-by-body pairs and 269.25 GiB of string search -- a BOUND rather than the work, because the loop breaks on the first body that mentions a script, so the realised cost is on the order of half that; it consumed over 4 minutes of CPU on its own against 51.54 seconds for an 8-file set without it. Indexing once turns that into a single pass. **NOT recommended without evidence first**: it changes a RATCHET, so its 2 verdict sets must be compared element-by-element before it is applied, because an optimisation that silently drops a match loosens the ratchet by the width of what it dropped.",
+      "**Raise the written limit to match the measurement.** Honest bookkeeping, but it concedes the header's own argument that a gate this expensive gets bypassed, and it would need raising again the next time the task list grows.",
+      "**Leave it.** The cost is real but bounded, and `sv` already derives its timeout from the measurement so the save path is protected. What this accepts is that the stated standard is decorative.",
+      "**Decide which files the gate should watch, not only how long it may take.** Cost and coverage trade directly: the gate costs 745 s and watches 120 of 785 test files. Tonight a 21-test guard on the tool that decides whether work stops was found red after 5 commits, because it is 1 of the 665 the gate does not run. Adding files raises the cost the first option would cap, so the 2 halves are 1 decision."
+    ],
+    "spoken_heading": "WHETHER THE COMMIT GATE SHOULD BE HELD TO ITS OWN STATED TIME LIMIT",
+    "title": "B81 — the commit gate costs more than the limit written in its own header",
+    "what": "Every commit runs a set of fast correctness tests first and refuses the commit if any are red. That set's own header states its design limit in these words: *\"A per-commit gate costing 11 minutes would be bypassed within a day, and a guard that gets bypassed is not a guard.\"* 11 minutes is 660 seconds. Measured across this session's 4 commits the gate took 800, 769, 738 and 745 seconds — 4 of 4 above the limit, mean 763.00, standard deviation 28.011902, and a one-sided t-test against 660 gives t = 7.354017 and p = 0.002598, agreed by SciPy and by an mpmath integration of the t density to 6 decimal places.",
+    "why": "The cause is structural rather than careless and the header says so itself: 1 of the guards is parametrised per completed task entry, so the gate's cost GROWS WITH THE TASK LIST. It is not a fixed price. The header's own measurement of 277 tests in a median 7.09 seconds was taken when the guard list held 7 files; it now holds about 125. What is missing is not the measurement, which exists and is accurate — exactly 2 files read the recorded duration, the hook that writes it and `sv` which consumes it, and nothing compares it against the limit. So the project has written down a standard, measured itself exceeding it, and has nothing that would ever say so."
