-  "recommendation_standards": "ADDITIVE: option 1 adds a reference to both surfaces and removes nothing; the spoken file gains a code he can speak and quote, the written twin keeps the full digest a machine needs. SIMPLEST SUFFICIENT: option 2 is complete and makes the spoken report materially worse for the one reader it exists for, at about 168 extra spoken tokens for 14 decisions. COMPOSABILITY: this IS the composed option, and the measurement is in the entry, 12 spoken tokens against 2, so the composition is justified on a named property rather than asserted.",
-  "spoken_heading": "WHICH IDENTIFIER A SPOKEN REPORT SHOULD CARRY",
-  "title": "B104 - no spoken report carries a ticket identifier, which is the facility he asked for",
-  "what": "He observed that he no longer sees the generated identifiers for issues, and that he only saw them for a short time after they were agreed. He is right. Measured over the files written today: 2 of 14 spoken text files carry at least 1 ticket identifier, 0.142857, Wilson 95 per cent [0.040094, 0.399414], Clopper-Pearson [0.017795, 0.428129]. The written twins are better and still thin at 11 of 33, 0.333333, Wilson [0.197502, 0.503921]. The master task list carries 80 distinct identifiers and every other note carries 3 or fewer.",
-  "why": "The obvious repair is hostile to the comfortable listening the spoken file exists for. A ticket identifier is 12 hexadecimal characters, and Firefox Read Aloud speaks it character by character, so 1 reference costs 12 spoken tokens against 2 for a short code. A report attaching an identifier to each of 14 decisions would add roughly 168 spoken tokens of hexadecimal to a document whose whole purpose is comfortable listening. A speakable identifier already exists and is his own: the short codes on the master task list, which are stable, unique, and already mapped to the store in both directions."
