- "task:B125": {
-  "evidence_line": "Producers: the chronology is from git, with the byte comparison and the heading positions agreeing on line 170 independently. The blind half's stored copy is 11,718 bytes, 170 lines and 5 headings and is identical for both seats; the file now carries 19,193 bytes, 223 lines and 7 headings; the joint half's store holds 0 copies of it. Full record: experimental_notes/Panel_FULL_RECORD_The_Period_Joint_2026-11-04.md",
-  "options": [
-   "**Have the joint brief quote the state of every file it references, as at the blind half's dispatch, composed with a refusal when a referenced file has moved since. This is the recommendation.** Quoting makes what each seat actually saw checkable afterwards, which is the same reason the brief already quotes the seats' replies word for word. The refusal catches the other order of events, where the brief was written first and the file changed afterwards. Neither half does both jobs, so this is a composed fix and the reason is named. The cost is a longer brief and 1 more thing that can refuse a dispatch.",
-   "**Only add the refusal.** Cheap and mechanical: compare each referenced file's hash against the blind half's copy and refuse to dispatch if they differ. It cannot see a file that was changed and then changed back, and it tells a seat nothing about what it should have been looking at.",
-   "**Only quote the files.** Makes the exposure verifiable without adding a way for a dispatch to fail, but a brief written before an edit still goes out carrying the older text with nothing to flag it.",
-   "**Leave it to discipline: do not edit a referenced file between the 2 halves.** No code at all. This is the weakest of the 4 because this session broke that discipline twice without noticing, which is how the defect was found."
-  ],
-  "recommendation_standards": "ADDITIVE: option 1 adds quoting and a refusal and removes nothing from the protocol. SIMPLEST SUFFICIENT: option 2 alone cannot see a file changed and changed back, and option 3 alone lets a stale brief go out unflagged, so neither single fix covers the failure the entry measured. COMPOSABILITY: THIS IS THE 1 DECISION HERE WHERE A COMPOSED FIX IS THE RECOMMENDATION, and it qualifies under his definition on a named property: quoting makes what each seat saw verifiable, the refusal makes a changed file stop the dispatch, and each alone leaves a measured gap the other closes. Option 4, discipline alone, is refuted by this session breaking it twice without noticing.",
-  "spoken_heading": "WHETHER A JOINT PANEL BRIEF SHOULD QUOTE THE FILES IT REFERENCES, OR REFUSE WHEN THEY HAVE MOVED",
-  "title": "B125 - a joint panel round asked 2 seats about a file that had changed since they answered, so their correct reading read as an error",
-  "what": "A panel round runs in 2 halves. In the blind half each seat gets its own copy of the repository and answers without seeing the other. In the joint half both replies are put in front of each other, and the brief for that half refers to files by their path. The problem is that a seat's copy is a photograph taken when its sandbox was built, while the joint brief is read against the files as they are now. On this round a file gained 2 new sections between the halves, 35 minutes and 318 minutes after the blind replies landed, and 1 of those sections was written after the test board finished. A seat correctly reported that something was absent from the copy it could see, and the brief's wording presented that correct answer as a mistake.",
-  "why": "It needs your ruling because all 3 remedies cost something different and the choice is yours. It also matters more than an ordinary stale reference: it turns a seat's correct work into what looks like an invention, and that is the 1 kind of error a panel cannot catch itself, because the seat has no way to know the file moved under it. The seat said it nearly reported itself for fabricating, and was stopped only by comparing the opening bytes of its own copy against the quoted text."
- },
